---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Adds friction for a casual copier while they are on your store", them: true, us: false },
  { label: "Changes anything about the text itself", them: false, us: false },
  { label: "Adds scripts to your storefront", them: true, us: false },
  { label: "Has any effect on Shopify's public feed surfaces", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Protection still exists after the text is pasted elsewhere", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Identifies a copy found on another site", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Produces an ownership document for a takedown", them: false, us: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="Kirpik vs copy and right-click blockers"
  description="Copy blockers, right-click disablers and devtools detectors act only inside a browser tab. Your descriptions also live in public feeds no script can reach. Run the test on any store, then see what invisible text watermarking does instead."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Technique explainer"
    title="Copy blockers vs invisible text watermarking"
    lead="Around seventy apps in Shopify's anti-theft category run a script that cancels right-click, text selection, copying or the developer tools. Here is what that script can genuinely do for your words, the one-minute test that shows where it stops, and what to put behind it."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What a blocker actually is</h2>
      <p>
        Install one of these apps and a small script joins your theme. While a visitor's browser runs
        that script, it cancels selection and clipboard events, swallows Ctrl+C and Ctrl+U, hides the
        context menu, and sometimes watches for the developer tools. That is the entire machine. It has
        no server component acting on your content and it changes nothing about the text itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some vendors say this plainly, and credit to them. CopyBlock's listing offers to "discourage
        casual copying on your storefront". Defendify calls itself "an extra layer of deterrence against
        casual content theft". Cozy AntiTheft says it "makes it harder to steal content". The developer of
        Content Protection by Webyze put it best in a public reply on its own listing: "there is no way
        to protect the content 100%, just ways to add hurdles." Those descriptions, from the vendors
        themselves in mid 2026, are accurate. Deterrence inside the tab is a real thing. It is just not
        protection of the text.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the tab is the boundary</h2>
      <p>
        A storefront script exists only on pages that load it. Shopify, by design, publishes your product
        data in several other places at the same time: the JSON feed at /products.json, per-product JSON
        endpoints, sitemaps, and RSS for blogs. These surfaces exist so software can read your catalogue,
        they cannot be switched off, and they are served without any storefront JavaScript running. A
        scraper or dropship importer reads them directly. It never sees the blocker, not because it
        defeated it, but because the blocker was never there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="trybox">
        <span class="eyebrow">Run the test yourself</span>
        <h2>Sixty seconds, no tools, any store</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Pick any Shopify store. One running a copy blocker makes the point best.</li>
          <li>Add <code>/products.json</code> to the end of its address and open it in a new tab.</li>
          <li>Read the response. Every product description is there in full, as plain text, with no
            right-click block, no selection block, and no script of any kind in the way.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          That page is what bulk copying actually looks like. Want to see your own store's version, plus
          the other public surfaces? <a href="/site-check/">Run the site check</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>The part that matters after the copy</h2>
      <p>
        Suppose the blocker does its job and a casual visitor gives up. The copies that still happen, via
        the feed, the page source, or a browser with scripts off, share one property: the words carry no
        trace of where they came from. Text is just characters. Whoever pastes it owns the argument until
        somebody proves otherwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the gap Kirpik fills, and it is a different job from blocking. Kirpik embeds an invisible
        algorithmic watermark, derived from your original text, into your descriptions, articles and
        pages. Nothing changes for readers or search engines, no script joins your theme, and your words
        stay exactly as written. When a copy turns up, detection reads your store's watermark code back
        out of it, and a Chain of Proof certificate turns the detection into a dated, verifiable
        ownership document.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can run a blocker and Kirpik together if the friction is worth it to you. Just be clear about
        which one is doing which job: the blocker inconveniences the person standing in your shop, the
        watermark follows the goods out the door.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <CompareTable themLabel="Copy blocker" rows={rows} />
      <p class="tnote">
        Blocker column reflects the typical storefront-script app in the anti-theft category, per the
        listings we surveyed in June and July 2026. Vendor quotes above are from the named apps' own
        public listings on those dates. App names and trademarks belong to their owners. Not legal advice.
        See <a href="/compare/">the full category survey</a> for app-by-app pages.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="Friction is optional. Proof is not."
    sub="Kirpik puts invisible, provable attribution inside the copy itself. Free to start, nothing added to your theme."
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .trybox { background: var(--amber-tint); border: 1px solid #ecd9b4; border-radius: var(--r-lg); padding: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.2rem); }
  .trybox h2 { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 1.1rem + 1.2vw, 1.9rem); margin-top: 0.8rem; }
  .trybox ol { margin-top: 1rem; padding-left: 1.4rem; display: grid; gap: 0.6rem; color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.65; }
  .trybox code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.9em; background: #fff; padding: 0.15em 0.45em; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .trybox p { margin-top: 1.1rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .trybox a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }

  .tnote { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; }
  .tnote a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
